<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kham is a historical region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, now divided mainly between the Tibet Autonomous Region and the Chinese provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, Qinghai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. A land of deep river gorges along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a>, it was long split among local kingdoms and chieftaincies such as Derge. Its people, the Khampa, were known as traders and warriors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kham.jpg" alt="Kham" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tibetischer_Kulturraum_Karte.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
